{"name":"Royal Goblet","hex":"#d4ca8e","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"royal","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Adjective","definitions":["Of or relating to a monarch or his (or her) family.","Having the air or demeanour of a monarch; illustrious; magnanimous; of more than common size or excellence."]},{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A royal person; a member of a royal family.","A standard size of printing paper, measuring 25 by 20 inches."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/royal"},{"term":"goblet","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A drinking vessel with a foot and stem."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/goblet"}],"entries":[{"title":"Chalice","blocks":[{"text":"A chalice is a drinking cup raised on a stem with a foot or base. Although it is a technical archaeological term, in modern parlance the word is now used almost exclusively for the cups used in Christian liturgy as part of a service of the Eucharist, such as a Catholic mass. These are normally made of metal, but neither the shape nor the material is a requirement. Most have no handles, and in recent centuries the cup at the top has usually been a simple flared shape."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/8f/German_-_Chalice_with_Saints_and_Scenes_from_the_Life_of_Christ_-_Walters_44116.jpg/330px-German_-_Chalice_with_Saints_and_Scenes_from_the_Life_of_Christ_-_Walters_44116.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Chalice","wikidata":"Q210723"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/70/Parts_of_a_liturgical_chalice.png/500px-Parts_of_a_liturgical_chalice.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/70/Parts_of_a_liturgical_chalice.png/500px-Parts_of_a_liturgical_chalice.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_c